<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Sulaymanid emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">814 CE – 931 CE (approx.) · capital Tlemcen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Alid dynasty related to the Idrisids that ruled Tlemcen and western Algeria in the 9th and 10th centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sulayman ibn Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> was a brother of Idris I, founder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a>. Around 814 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> to Sulayman's son Muhammad, establishing a branch dynasty, the Sulaymanids, which ruled the city and surrounding region, formerly held by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banu-ifran-emirate">Banu Ifran emirate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known in detail. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was divided among family members, who ruled towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, Arshgul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenes">Ténès</a>, relying on local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanata-berbers">Zenata Berber</a> tribes. Like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisids</a>, the Sulaymanids drew prestige from descent from the Prophet's family, and Tlemcen prospered as a crossroads of trade routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 10th century the Sulaymanid princes were caught in the struggle between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba</a>. Fatimid forces ended their rule around 931, and several family members fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 814 CE – 931 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.90%;width:2.33%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>814 CE – 931 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tlemcen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Sulayman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sulaymanids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=872&amp;focus=sulaymanid-emirate" aria-label="Sulaymanid emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4647 915 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sulaymanid emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4967 1171L4873 1200M4967 1171L5811 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4873 1200h0M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4967 1171h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4967 1171h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tlemcen. <a href="/atlas?year=872&amp;focus=sulaymanid-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
